WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:04.551
In this lesson we will see how to find the
angle

2
00:00:04.551 --> 00:00:07.320
between a line and a plane, and also

3
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:14.200
how to determine whether a line and a plane in
space are parallel or perpendicular.

4
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:22.040
Let a plane Q and a line L intersect in space.
The plane is described by its normal

5
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:28.380
vector n, and s is the direction vector of the
line. To find

6
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.718
the angle between the line and the plane,

7
00:00:30.718 --> 00:00:34.140
the sine of the angle between the normal
vector of the plane

8
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:40.400
and the direction vector of the line is found
by this formula.

9
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.631
Let us look at an example. Let the equation of
a plane

10
00:00:44.631 --> 00:00:47.520
and the canonical equation of a line be given.

11
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.940
We write the coordinates of the plane's normal

12
00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.840
vector and the line's direction vector.

13
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.570
Now let us find the angle between the line and
the plane.

14
00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.042
We substitute the given data into the formula
and do

15
00:01:01.042 --> 00:01:04.380
the calculations in the numerator and
denominator,

16
00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:07.859
and after cancelling we get that the sine of
the

17
00:01:07.859 --> 00:01:11.120
angle equals three divided by the square root

18
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.980
of one hundred seventy-eight.

19
00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.769
To determine whether a line and a plane are
parallel

20
00:01:20.769 --> 00:01:23.160
or perpendicular, we use their essential

21
00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:29.680
components: the normal vector n and the
direction vector s.

22
00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:36.710
Let us draw a plane Q and a line L parallel to
it. Now we draw the normal vector n

23
00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:43.300
and the direction vector s. If the line L is
parallel to the plane Q,

24
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:49.940
then vectors n and s are perpendicular, that
is,

25
00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.780
the dot product of the vectors is zero.

26
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.455
Let us look at an example: from the given
equations we need

27
00:01:58.455 --> 00:02:01.450
to check whether the plane and the line are
parallel.

28
00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:08.280
We write the coordinates of the normal and
direction vectors

29
00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:14.620
and substitute them into the perpendicularity
condition for the vectors, getting

30
00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:18.440
zero, so the line and the plane are parallel.

31
00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:27.160
Let us draw a plane Q and a line L. Now we
draw the normal vector n

32
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:33.830
and the direction vector s. If vectors n and s

33
00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:40.530
are parallel, the line L is perpendicular to
the plane Q,

34
00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:45.030
that is, the coordinates of the vectors are
proportional to each other.

35
00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:53.340
Let us look at an example: from the given
equations we need to check

36
00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.984
whether the plane and the line are
perpendicular.

37
00:02:55.984 --> 00:02:58.790
We write the coordinates of the normal and
direction

38
00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:05.450
vectors and substitute them into the
parallelism condition for the vectors.

39
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:12.030
We check that the coordinates are proportional:
all the fractions equal one third,

40
00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:15.790
so the plane and the line are perpendicular.

41
00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:23.600
To sum up: to determine the relative position
of a line and a plane in space,

42
00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:29.880
we use the components of these objects: the
normal vector of the plane

43
00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.680
and the direction vector of the line.
